{"candidates":[{"content":"Artifact 18174034ea0c contradicts artifact 2f8f85328fae on the locus of the correctness obligation for deterministic replay in an append-only log: 18174034ea0c asserts the writer must be idempotent, while 2f8f85328fae asserts the writer need only append and the consumer's fold operation must be commutative (duplicate-suppressing). They share a mechanism—duplicate suppression—but allocate it to different components. The contradiction is sharpened by 3fc33b3603be's formal definition of idempotency as f(f(x))=f(x), which 18174034ea0c applies to the writer while 2f8f85328fae effectively relocates to the fold. This is refuted if the two positions are equivalent reformulations rather than competing allocations—i.e., if writer-level idempotency and consumer-level fold commutativity always co-occur as jointly necessary and sufficient conditions, making the disagreement merely notational rather than substantive.","evidence_refs":[{"block":"18174034ea0c22d5","quote":"For deterministic replay in an append-only log, the required property is idempotency because the log's determinism is an outcome property, not merely a harm-avoidance property."},{"block":"2f8f85328fae446","quote":"An append-only event log requires neither idempotence nor safety from its writers in the conventional sense; it requires commutativity of the fold operation over the event stream."},{"block":"3fc33b3603be432","quote":"An idempotent operation satisfies f(f(x))=f(x): repeated application yields the same result as a single application."}],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"2f8f85328fae446c3c422bd4286bfcd4df6abfd26babf3005820e939993c4d0b"},{"role":"dependence","target":"3fc33b3603be4326107111e73ee5bfa735e6f92a6da6a64286fc895870602e42"}],"typicality":0.62}],"simulation_proposals":[]}